# Overview
#
# A contrastive `while` sets one claim against another (`reads are fast,
# while the writes wait for the lock`). `while` also marks time (`while
# the lock is held`), so a reader who meets it after a comma must decide
# which of the two it marks. The fix writes `but` for a contrast or
# splits the sentence. The rule sits at Recommended, because a `while`
# after a comma can also mark time.
#
# Expression
#
# The pattern matches `while` after a comma and before a determiner, a
# pronoun, or a proper noun. A determiner is a word that introduces a
# noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. A proper noun is a name that
# takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. Harper's part-of-speech
# tagger marks each word with a tag for its word class. The pattern
# names the three classes as `DET` (a determiner), `PRON` (a pronoun),
# and `PROPN` (a proper noun). A `while` before any other word passes,
# even when it marks a contrast (`reads are fast, while writes are
# slow`).
#
# By default, a Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"`
# lets the pattern cross one.
#
# False Positives
#
# `while` with no comma before it passes, because the rule takes the
# comma as the sign of a contrast. A `while` after a comma that marks
# time still draws a finding (`It is served anyway, while the refresher
# catches up`). That sentence reads one way after the comma goes.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 50 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits marked a contrast.
expr main , (while [DET, PRON, PROPN])
let message "Contrastive `while`. Write `but` for a contrast, or split the sentence."
let description "Flags `while` after a comma, where it usually marks a contrast that `but` marks in one reading."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The hook waits while the lock is held."
